WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.490
March 10 was bonus day for the 8,000 employees
of Silicon Valley Bank.

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:06.549
They got their bonus that Friday.

00:00:06.549 --> 00:00:09.380
Hours later, the bank failed.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:14.460
On Monday, customers could be seen lining
up to try to retrieve their funds.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:18.770
Silicon Valley Bank was the bank of choice
for thousands of companies in the tech sector

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:22.710
- many of them startups fueled by angel investors.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:26.981
Almost half of all venture capital funding
in the United States flowed through the bank

00:00:26.981 --> 00:00:29.000
at one time or another.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.079
And then, suddenly, it all came to an end.

00:00:32.079 --> 00:00:37.700
The speed at which things unraveled caught
many by surprise – even the bank’s CEO.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:43.400
A few weeks before the collapse, CEO Greg
Becker told CNBC that the future looked good.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:48.840
“What we are expecting to see in 2023, first
half, actually in venture capital, actually

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.449
a little bit more of a decline even than what
we saw in the fourrth quarter.

00:00:52.449 --> 00:00:56.989
But the second half was going to kind of create
that modest improvement and really set the

00:00:56.989 --> 00:00:59.269
stage for a better 2024.

00:00:59.269 --> 00:01:04.449
So, we’re optimistic because our crystal
ball is a little clearer than it was in the

00:01:04.449 --> 00:01:05.580
third quarter last year.”

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:10.640
Perhaps his crystal ball wasn’t shiny enough
to predict the future.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.900
Startups struggled to raise additional venture
capital due to the current economic environment.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:20.280
So, they began withdrawing more and more of
their cash from their SVB accounts.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.400
The bank had the money – in theory – but
it wasn’t sitting in a vault waiting to

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.270
be handed back to its customers.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:31.490
You’re thinking of this all wrong as if
I had the money back in the safe.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:32.490
The money’s not here.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:35.870
The bank had invested the money in Treasury
bonds, a type of government debt, in which

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:39.450
the government borrows money and pays it back
with interest.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:44.040
When customers started pulling their money
out of the bank, SVB had no choice but to

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:49.090
start selling the bonds at a discount so they
could get enough cash to satisfy customer

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:50.320
demand.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:56.020
Becker decided to publicly announce that the
bank had sold off $2 1 billion in bonds.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:02:00.770
But those bonds weren't worth as much as they
used to be because the Fed kept raising interest

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:01.770
rates.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:05.700
When interest rates go up, bond prices usually
go down.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.489
This is because new bonds have higher interest
rates, so people prefer them over older bonds.

00:02:10.489 --> 00:02:16.519
Think of it like this: Imagine you have a
candy store and sell a candy for $1.

00:02:16.519 --> 00:02:20.410
This candy gives the buyer a little 10-cent
surprise each year.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:25.500
If another store sells a similar candy for
the same price but offers a 15-cent surprise

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:29.569
each year, people would choose to buy candy
from the other store instead.

00:02:29.569 --> 00:02:35.390
So the bank had to sell its older bonds at
lower prices, which ultimately led to a loss

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:37.640
of $1.8 billion.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:42.110
While Becker hoped to raise more than that
in new capital, he said he still had not found

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:45.349
anyone willing to help the bank ride out the
storm.

00:02:45.349 --> 00:02:49.080
Perhaps he was looking to be transparent,
but it backfired.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:50.380
Big time.

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:56.390
CNN quotes an anonymous bank employee as saying:
“That was absolutely idiotic…their transparency

00:02:56.390 --> 00:02:59.190
and forthrightness did them in.”

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:03.690
The extent of the bank’s financial troubles
panicked customers, leading to a phenomenon

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:08.990
called a bank run, where everyone starts taking
their money out of the bank all at once.

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:15.319
The next day, customers withdrew $42 billion
from their accounts, leaving SVB with a negative

00:03:15.319 --> 00:03:18.590
cash balance of $958 million.

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:24.660
And, public filings show Becker himself cashed
out personal shares and options worth $2 million

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:27.360
dollars just before the collapse!

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:33.170
The day the bank failed, Becker told employees,
“I’m not making the decisions anymore.”

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:38.299
This is the second largest bank failure in
US history, behind only the collapse of Washington

00:03:38.299 --> 00:03:42.710
Mutual at the height of the subprime mortgage
crisis in 2008.

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:48.560
And then, the third largest bank failure in
U.S. history happened on SVB’s heels.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:53.890
Two days later, on March 12, regulators shut
down Signature Bank in New York to prevent

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:57.569
the crisis from spreading through the entire
financial system.

00:03:57.569 --> 00:04:02.000
A quarter of Signature Bank’s deposits came
from the cryptocurrency sector.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:07.349
But, regulators denied it closed the bank
because of its large hand in crypto and, rather,

00:04:07.349 --> 00:04:12.599
cited what it called “a significant crisis
of confidence in the bank’s leadership”.

00:04:12.599 --> 00:04:15.760
The question now is: are there more bank failures
to come?

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:18.790
They are more common than people think.

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:26.270
Few people are old enough to remember the
2,293 banks that collapsed in 1931, a development

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:28.440
that led to the Great Depression.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:33.620
But there have been more than 500 since the
collapse of Washington Mutual in 2008, although

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:37.270
most pale in comparison to the size of SVBs.

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:42.300
Mark Williams, a former bank examiner for
the Federal Reserve, told Boston University’s

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:49.190
daily news website BU Today, “If not quelled,
bank runs can spread like wildfire, crippling

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:51.850
even the soundest of banks.”

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:55.260
To quell further panic, President Biden quickly
assured Americans.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:05:00.419
“Americans can have confidence that the
banking system is safe.

00:05:00.419 --> 00:05:03.009
Your deposits will be there when you need
them.”

00:05:03.009 --> 00:05:08.199
In the U.S., deposits in a bank are insured
by the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation

00:05:08.199 --> 00:05:11.530
or FDIC up to $250,000.

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:17.050
However, over 90% of deposits at SVB exceeded
that amount.

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:22.120
The federal government then jumped in to guarantee
deposits greater than $250,000.

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:26.840
And, the Federal Reserve also said it would
make additional emergency funds available

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:32.540
to eligible financial institutions to prevent
similar problems in the future.

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:35.880
Billionaire hedge fund investor, Bill Ackman,
made a point of saying:

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:41.650
“Had [U.S. regulators] not intervened today,
we would have had a 1930s bank run continuing

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:47.600
first thing Monday causing enormous economic
damage and hardship to millions.”

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:53.370
However, Ackman also warned: “More banks
will likely fail despite the intervention”.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:59.289
There’s little doubt that the Fed is partly
to blame for SVB’s stunning collapse.

00:05:59.289 --> 00:06:03.949
Over the past year, the Federal Reserve has
been aggressively hiking interest rates to

00:06:03.949 --> 00:06:09.820
tame inflation by making borrowing more expensive,
which slows down spending and keeps prices

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:11.199
from rising too quickly.

00:06:11.199 --> 00:06:15.960
And in the process, SVB got caught in the
undertow.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:21.700
Former U.S. Treasury Secretary Larry Summers
criticized regulators and also the bank’s

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:22.700
management.

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:27.660
“And I have to say that it doesn’t appear
on current facts that a very good job was

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:34.120
done about regulating and supervising Silicon
Valley Bank.

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:40.639
It had committed kind of the most elementary
errors, in borrowing money in a very short

00:06:40.639 --> 00:06:46.789
term way and then investing it in a long-term
way, and then when interest rates went up,

00:06:46.789 --> 00:06:49.139
those assets lost their value.”

00:06:49.139 --> 00:06:54.200
Some are blaming wokeness for the bank’s
demise, implying that the collapse was related

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:57.660
to the company’s focus on diversity and
inclusion.

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:02.790
A Wall Street Journal opinion columnist wrote:
"I'm not saying 12 white men would have avoided

00:07:02.790 --> 00:07:07.750
this mess, but the company may have been distracted
by diversity demands"

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:12.240
The best outcome for the failed bank is for
U.S. officials to find a buyer.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:17.819
There will be no bailout…which typically
uses taxpayer dollars to keep the bank afloat.

00:07:17.819 --> 00:07:22.440
Biden does not want to reignite the anger
from the 2008 Wall Street bailouts that were

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:27.620
funded by taxpayers, especially with a presidential
election approaching.

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:31.800
The collapse of Silicon Valley Bank brings
up memories of another President who took

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:36.949
office in 1933 when banks were going under
almost daily.

00:07:36.949 --> 00:07:42.590
During his inaugural address, President Franklin
D. Roosevelt famously urged people not to

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:43.590
panic.

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:52.800
“Let me assert my firm belief that the only
thing we have to fear is fear itself—nameless,

00:07:52.800 --> 00:08:03.080
unreasoning, unjustified terror which paralyzes
needed efforts to convert retreat into advance.”

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:10.570
It appears that 90 years later, Roosevelt's
wise counsel has been largely ignored.

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:16.310
Financial stocks have now lost a combined
$465 billion in just one weekend.

00:08:16.310 --> 00:08:18.639
But will it be the last disaster?

00:08:18.639 --> 00:08:23.790
This crash was caused in part by the Fed raising
rates - which they may still be committed

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:24.790
to.

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:32.200
JP Morgan says the S&amp;P 500 could sink another
20% as the economy is pushed to the brink.

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:37.110
So big institutions like them are going big
into alternative assets

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:44.470
One alternative asset rose an average of 29%
last year, handily beating stocks: fine art

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:48.589
Masterworks offers fully investible art from
legends like Picasso,

00:08:48.589 --> 00:08:53.180
Which are qualified with the SEC, and broken
into shares - so you don't need millions of

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:55.120
dollars to invest.

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:59.640
In fact, each of their 11 exits to date has
returned a profit.

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:02.930
As a result, paintings have sold out in hours,
not days

00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:08.699
But right now you can get priority access
to skip the wait list below.

